  "step_3": "The decision reason focuses on skewness, defined as the relative concentration of probability on favorable outcomes. In this case, Lottery A has twice the probability of a favorable outcome (0.2% vs 0.1%), making it more left-skewed, and thus preferred by the reason. The individual also focuses on the higher chance (doubled probability) of winning 3000€, explicitly using this as their justification after ruling out expected value. While they do not use the term 'skewness', their reasoning aligns with the core idea of preferring a lottery with a relatively higher probability on a favorable outcome, especially when the expected values are equal. The information they consider (probabilities of winning, magnitude of win, and equality of expected value) overlaps with the relevant information for the skewness reason. Their justification—preferring the higher chance of a good outcome—mirrors the skewness reason’s rule. Therefore, although the terminology differs, the cognitive process matches the decision reason closely.",
